ONE MORE THING THIS FILE DOES NOT COVER, ADDED 2026-10-17 BECAUSE IT WAS
MISSED AND IT IS NOT A LICENSING QUESTION AT ALL:

  A LICENCE IS NOT THE ONLY THING THAT CONSTRAINS DISTRIBUTION. libsodium's
  ISC licence imposes nothing beyond attribution, and yet integrating it
  changes what we must file with a government before shipping, because the
  app now provides content confidentiality with a bundled crypto library.
  That is US EXPORT CONTROL (EAR Category 5 Part 2), not copyright, and it is
  recorded in SECTION 0's libsodium entry and as risk R-33. Reviewing a
  dependency's licence and concluding "clear to ship" is, for a cryptographic
  dependency, the wrong conclusion from the right evidence.

Three rules govern this file from Phase 1 onward:

  1. From Phase 1, this file MUST be GENERATED BY CI from the actual dependency
     link graph and the vendored licence texts -- never maintained by hand.
     A hand-maintained notices file drifts from reality, and a notices file that
     does not match the shipped binary provides no legal protection.

  2. CI fails the build if any directory under third_party/ lacks a LICENSE file,
     a pinned commit hash, a SHA-256, or an entry here. (NFR-8)

  3. Every entry below is PROVISIONAL. Licences change between versions --
     Anti-Grain Geometry went from permissive (2.4) to GPL (2.5), and OpenCV moved
     from BSD-3 to Apache-2.0 at 4.5.0. Each licence MUST be re-verified against
     the exact pinned upstream revision at integration time, and the full set
     reviewed by counsel before Phase 5 (PDF) and Phase 7 (OCR).

Distribution model assumed throughout: a closed-source commercial application,
with a statically linked native core, distributed via the Apple App Store and
Google Play.

