  NOT A LICENSING OBLIGATION, BUT A DISTRIBUTION ONE -- AND IT IS NEW
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Integrating libsodium makes the app's encryption NON-EXEMPT for Apple's
  export-compliance purposes, and this file would be misleading if it stopped
  at "ISC, clear to ship".

  Apple's exemptions cover, in substance: encryption provided BY THE
  OPERATING SYSTEM and called through Apple's own APIs; HTTPS/TLS to one's
  own backend; and encryption used ONLY for authentication, signing or
  integrity. Our case matches none of them -- the encryption is in a bundled
  third-party library and it provides CONTENT CONFIDENTIALITY, which
  ADR-0002 decision 1 makes the product's headline property. So
  ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption = YES, the app falls under EAR Category 5
  Part 2, and License Exception ENC (15 CFR 740.17(b)(1)) requires a
  SELF-CLASSIFICATION REPORT emailed to BIS and to the NSA encryption
  request coordinator before export AND ANNUALLY THEREAFTER. France
  separately requires a declaration.

  Three precisions, because each is easy to get wrong in the opposite
  direction:

    1. THIS IS NOT A libsodium PROBLEM. It follows from ADR-0002 decision 1.
       Any library that encrypts user content lands in the same place;
       choosing Monocypher or writing our own would not avoid it.
    2. Using Apple's CryptoKit instead would probably NOT avoid it, and the
       widespread belief that it would is a misreading of the OS-provided
       exemption. That question needs counsel, and in any case CryptoKit is
       ruled out on portability grounds (ADR-0001's C/C++ core must derive
       the same key on iOS, Android and desktop).
    3. IT IS NOT AN ENGINEERING DELIVERABLE. It is a release-process one with
       a recurring annual component, and nothing in this repo owns it.
       docs/security-model.md section 12 covers supply chain and is silent on
       export control; deployment.md is silent too.

  Recorded as risk R-33 in docs/risk-register.md. Owner: product lead.
  Trigger: the first App Store submission containing security/crypto.

